.TH CRUCIBLE\-TUI 1 "2026-09-07" "crucible-tui" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
crucible\-tui \- terminal markdown viewer (installed command: \fBmdview\fR)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mdview
.I file.md
[\fB\-\-width\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-pager\fR]
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-ascii\fR|\fB\-\-unicode\fR]
[\fB\-\-color\fR[=\fIWHEN\fR]]
[\fB\-\-no\-color\fR]
[\fB\-\-plain\fR]
[\fB\-\-hyperlinks\fR[=\fIWHEN\fR]]
[\fB\-\-probe\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-licenses\fR]
.br
.B mdview view
.I file.md
[\fIoptions\fR ...]
.br
.B mdview highlight\-as
.I fence
.I language
.br
.B mdview probe
[\fB\-\-refresh\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-cache\fR]
[\fB\-\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR]
.br
.B mdview help
[\fIsubcommand\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mdview
renders a markdown file to the terminal: frontmatter as a metadata block,
wide tables wrapped losslessly \(em no cell is ever truncated, and columns
shrink proportionally to a floor, though a table too wide even at that floor
emits at its minimum feasible width and can still exceed the terminal width
\(em loose and tight lists reflowed correctly at the target width, and fenced
code passed through byte\-for\-byte.
.PP
A bare
.I file.md
argument is, and remains, a complete invocation \(em no subcommand is ever
required to view a file. Subcommands below sit alongside that default, not in
front of it.
.PP
The repository and project are named
.B crucible\-tui ;
the installed command, its environment variables, and its config directory
are all
.B mdview ,
and that split is settled, not an unfinished rename.
.B mdview
describes what the tool does today \(em it views markdown \(em and
.B crucible\-tui
is the name of the project the other TUI\-application functions (editing, a
file tree) would belong to if they arrive; that is the event which would
justify renaming the command, not this page. This page installs as both
.BR mdview (1)
and
.BR crucible\-tui (1),
so either name resolves to the same manual.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.BI "view " "file.md " "[options...]"
Spelled\-out form of the default action: render
.I file.md
and print it, exactly as a bare
.I file.md
argument does. See OPTIONS below.
.TP
.BI "highlight\-as " "fence language"
Map a fenced code block's declared language
.RI ( fence ,
e.g. the word after the opening \(oq```\(cq) to a language the syntax
highlighter recognizes
.RI ( language ),
and save that mapping to the user config file (see FILES). The mapping is
validated before it is written: an unrecognized
.I language
is rejected with an explanatory message rather than being written silently,
which would otherwise leave the fence unhighlighted with no indication why.
Two mappings are built in and need no configuration:
.B dataviewjs
renders as
.B javascript
and
.B dataview
renders as
.BR sql .
A configured mapping for a fence language that already has a built\-in
default overrides it. Only the one mapping named on the command line is
touched; every other key in the config file, and every other
.B highlight\-as
mapping already recorded, is left exactly as it was.
.TP
.BI probe " [\-\-refresh] [\-\-no\-cache] [\-\-timeout " "MS" "]"
Report how this terminal renders East Asian
.B Ambiguous
characters, and exit. Almost every non\-ASCII character in ordinary prose
belongs to that class \(em the dashes \(en \(em, the arrows, the ellipsis, the
comparison signs, the bullet, and the whole box\-drawing set \(em and Unicode
defines it as rendering at either one or two columns depending on the
terminal, with no way to tell from the character itself. A viewer that
guesses wrong wraps every line one or two columns off.
.IP
The probe writes one test character at the cursor, asks the terminal for the
cursor position before and after it with a Device Status Report
.RB ( "ESC [ 6 n" ),
and takes the difference. The reply is read in raw mode with a short timeout;
cooked mode is restored on every exit path, including an interrupt or a
.BR kill (1),
and the test character is erased afterwards, so the probe leaves nothing on
screen. It runs only when standard input and standard output are both
terminals \(em never under
.BR \-\-check ,
never in developer tooling, never when piped.
.IP
The answer is cached per terminal identity (see FILES), so it is measured once
per terminal rather than once per invocation, and a second terminal never
inherits the first one's answer.
.B \-\-refresh
ignores the cached answer and probes again;
.B \-\-no\-cache
probes without recording the result;
.B \-\-timeout
sets how long to wait for the reply, in milliseconds (default 200).
.B \-\-probe
on the default action is an accepted spelling of the same thing.
.TP
.BI "help " "[subcommand]"
Print this usage information, or \(em given a subcommand name \(em that
subcommand's own usage. Equivalent to
.B \-\-help
or
.B \-h
passed to the subcommand directly.
.SH OPTIONS
These apply to the default (bare\-file, or explicit
.BR view )
action.
.TP
.BI "\-\-width " N
Target render width, in columns. Default: the terminal's reported column
count if available, otherwise 100 \(em chosen so headless or redirected runs
stay deterministic.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-pager
Write plain output to standard output instead of piping it through a pager.
Implied whenever standard output is not a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Render with no pager and exit non\-zero if any non\-fenced\-code line exceeds
the target width. This is the tool's own self\-smoke gate; fenced code is
exempt because it is never rewritten (see FILES and the fence\-language
discussion above \(em the same "never rewrite what was authored" principle
applies to code blocks generally).
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
Force the ASCII glyph tier for bullets, rules, table borders and task states,
regardless of what auto\-detection would otherwise choose.
.TP
.B \-\-unicode
Force the Unicode glyph tier. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-ascii .
Ignored, with a notice on standard error, when the ambiguous\-width setting is
.B wide
\(em see
.B MDVIEW_AMBIGUOUS
under ENVIRONMENT for why the Unicode tier cannot be laid out in that case.
.TP
.BR \-\-color [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
When to emit colour:
.BR auto " (the default), " always " or " never .
A bare
.B \-\-color
means
.BR \-\-color=always .
Given more than once, the last occurrence wins. An unrecognized
.I WHEN
is an error, unlike the same value in
.B MDVIEW_COLOR
\(em a typo on the command line can be corrected now, where a typo in an
exported variable would otherwise refuse every document for the rest of that
shell's life. See
.B MDVIEW_COLOR
under ENVIRONMENT for the full resolution order.
.IP
An explicit
.B never
directive is not the same as byte\-silence: it means
.IR "no hue" .
Bold, dim, italic, underline and strikethrough are still emitted; only the
seven colour codes are dropped. True byte\-silence \(em nothing of this
tool's own emitted at all \(em comes only from a kill\-switch
.RB ( NO_COLOR
present, or
.BR FORCE_COLOR=0 ),
a non\-terminal destination, or
.BR \-\-plain .
One consequence: fenced\-code syntax highlighting runs under full colour
only, so under any
.B never
directive a code fence renders with no highlighting at all \(em the same
bytes a fence gets under true colourless output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Alias for
.BR \-\-color=never .
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Emit nothing of this tool's own: no SGR colour codes, no OSC\-8 hyperlinks.
Explicit and absolute \(em it overrides every other colour and hyperlink
input, including a malformed
.B MDVIEW_COLOR
or
.BR MDVIEW_HYPERLINKS ,
neither of which is even read under
.B \-\-plain
(so no warning about either is printed). Escape bytes already present in the
document being viewed are content, not this tool's output, and pass through
unchanged \(em exactly as they do under
.BR \-\-color=never .
.TP
.BR \-\-hyperlinks [= \fIWHEN\fR ]
When to emit OSC\-8 hyperlinks:
.BR auto " (the default), " always " or " never .
Same conventions as
.B \-\-color
above: a bare
.B \-\-hyperlinks
means
.BR \-\-hyperlinks=always ,
the last occurrence wins, and an unrecognized
.I WHEN
is an error. See
.B MDVIEW_HYPERLINKS
under ENVIRONMENT for how this combines with the non\-TTY ceiling, which
stays absolute regardless of this flag.
Under tmux or GNU screen (any
.B TERM
beginning
.B screen
or
.BR tmux ),
the OSC\-8 sequence is wrapped in a DCS passthrough so it survives the
multiplexer.
.TP
.B \-\-probe
Run the terminal ambiguous\-width diagnostic and exit, exactly as
.B mdview probe
does. Takes no file argument.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit. At the top level (no subcommand named), this includes
the subcommand list; run
.B mdview help
.I subcommand
or
.B mdview
.I subcommand
.B \-\-help
for one subcommand's own usage.
.TP
.B \-\-licenses
Print the aggregated third\-party license notices for everything compiled
into the binary \(em the Rust crate closure, the vendored syntax grammars, and
the vendored notices for ported code and data \(em and exit. Takes no file argument. The same text is generated by
.B scripts/gen\-licenses.ts
into
.I THIRD\-PARTY\-LICENSES.txt
in the source repository.
.SH WIKI\-LINKS
Obsidian\-style
.B [[slug]]
syntax is recognized directly in the lexer's own pass, not bolted on
afterward.
.BR [[slug]]
renders
.I slug
as the label;
.BR [[slug|Alias]]
renders
.I Alias
instead, with any inline markup inside the label itself rendered;
.B [[slug#fragment|Alias]]
is the same with the fragment discarded from the rendered label; and
.B ![[target]]
is an embed, rendered as
.IR "embed: target" .
The label renders cyan and underlined under the resolved colour policy (see
OPTIONS above); a URL\-shaped slug emits a well\-formed OSC\-8 hyperlink
under the same conditions a markdown link does.
.PP
Because recognition happens inside the lexer's own pass, verbatim contexts
are literal by construction: fenced and indented code, block HTML, a formed
codespan, and a link or image destination or title never turn
.B [[...]]
into a wiki\-link. Inside a table cell, a wiki\-link's label is styled and
whitespace\-normalized like any other cell text; an unescaped
.B |
inside
.B [[...]]
splits the cell per GFM table rules, so the authored\-correct form for a
label needing a literal pipe is
.BR \e| .
A
.B [[...]]
written inside a markdown link's label, or inside an image's alt text, no
longer forms the outer link or image \(em the wiki\-link wins.
.PP
On a hyperlink\-capable terminal with hyperlinks on, a
.B [[slug]]
naming a file under the note's vault or repository root becomes a
.B file://
OSC\-8 hyperlink the terminal can open. The root is the nearest ancestor
directory of the viewed file containing
.B .obsidian
or
.BR .git ,
else the file's own directory. The slug is resolved first as a path
relative to that root (with or without
.BR .md ),
then as a unique basename anywhere under it, ties broken by fewest path
segments and then by the lexicographically smaller path; matching tries the
exact case first, then case\-insensitively. A slug that resolves to nothing
renders exactly as before: styled, unlinked. Wiki\-links inside table cells
are never linked. This link sits on the same
.B \-\-hyperlinks
axis as a markdown link, so
.BR \-\-plain ,
.B \-\-hyperlinks=never
and piped output emit none of it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mdview/config.json
(or, if
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset,
.IR ~/.config/mdview/config.json )
User configuration, a JSON object. Five keys:
.RS
.TP
.B emoji
.RB ( true " or " false )
Overrides the glyph\-tier default for
.B :shortcode:
substitution (Unicode substitutes a glyph, ASCII keeps the literal
shortcode). Unset follows the tier.
.TP
.B color
.RB ( auto ", " always " or " never )
A standing colour preference, below both
.B \-\-color
and
.B MDVIEW_COLOR
in the order given under ENVIRONMENT.
.TP
.B hyperlinks
.RB ( auto ", " always " or " never )
A standing hyperlink preference, its own axis independent of
.BR color .
Below both
.B \-\-hyperlinks
and
.B MDVIEW_HYPERLINKS
and above terminal detection, in the order given under ENVIRONMENT; the
non\-TTY ceiling and
.B \-\-plain
both still outrank it absolutely.
.TP
.B highlightAs
An object mapping a fence language to a highlighter language, written by
.B mdview highlight\-as
(see SUBCOMMANDS above) and layered over the built\-in
.B dataviewjs
/
.B dataview
defaults \(em a configured entry for a language that also has a built\-in
default takes precedence over it.
.TP
.B corpusManifest
Developer tooling: points the corpus sweep at a manifest file. Not consulted
by normal rendering.
.RE
.IP
A missing config file is normal and silently equivalent to an empty one. A
config file that exists but cannot be parsed, or whose
.B highlightAs
value is the wrong shape, is never treated as fatal and never reported as a
raw parser stack trace: the problem is named in one line on standard error
(the file's path and what was wrong with it), and rendering continues using
whatever in the file was still usable, falling back further to built\-in
defaults for anything that was not.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/mdview/ambiguous\-width.json
(or, if
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
is unset,
.IR ~/.cache/mdview/ambiguous\-width.json )
Cached results of
.BR "mdview probe" ,
one entry per terminal identity. The identity is composed from
.BR TERM ,
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
.BR COLORTERM ,
.BR VTE_VERSION ,
the terminal multiplexer in use, and the locale \(em deliberately more than
.B TERM
alone:
.B TERM
is a terminfo name rather than an identity, so several unrelated terminal
emulators can report the same generic value, and
.B VTE_VERSION
is the field that actually tells them apart. Multiplexers do not agree on
whether they rewrite
.BR TERM :
tmux does; zellij does not, passing the host's own value through unchanged,
which is why the multiplexer field is also part of the key. A terminal's
ambiguous\-width behaviour is, in any case, conventionally locale\-driven.
Entirely derived state: delete the file and the next probe recreates it. A
corrupt or unreadable cache is treated as an empty one, never as an error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MDVIEW_AMBIGUOUS
.RB ( narrow " or " wide )
How wide East Asian
.B Ambiguous
characters are on this terminal. Overrides everything else, including a
cached probe result. Any other value \(em including an empty one or a typo
\(em is ignored rather than rejected, exactly as for
.BR MDVIEW_GLYPHS .
.IP
Resolution order, highest first: this variable; a cached probe result for this
terminal; a live probe, when standard input and standard output are both
terminals; and otherwise
.BR narrow ,
which is what Unicode Standard Annex #11 itself recommends when the context
cannot be established.
.IP
.B wide
forces the ASCII glyph tier, overriding
.B \-\-unicode
and
.BR MDVIEW_GLYPHS .
This is arithmetic rather than preference: the Unicode tier's bullet, rule and
box\-drawing characters are themselves ambiguous, so under
.B wide
a table's horizontal border is drawn at exactly twice the width its columns
were allocated for, and no re\-allocation can recover it. Every ASCII
substitute is unambiguously one column. Set
.B MDVIEW_AMBIGUOUS=narrow
to get the box drawing back and accept the overflow.
.TP
.B MDVIEW_COLOR
.RB ( auto ", " always " or " never )
Whether to emit colour. An unrecognized or empty value is ignored with one
line on standard error rather than rejected, exactly as for
.BR MDVIEW_GLYPHS .
Under
.BR \-\-plain ,
this variable is never read at all, so a malformed value produces no warning
either.
.IP
Resolution order, highest first:
.B \-\-plain
(absolute \(em see OPTIONS above);
.BR \-\-color ;
this variable;
.BR NO_COLOR ;
.BR FORCE_COLOR ;
the config file's
.B color
key; and finally whether standard output is a terminal. Piped output is
therefore colourless unless colour is asked for explicitly.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Present at all \(em including set to an empty string \(em means never emit
colour. See
.IR https://no-color.org .
It outranks
.BR FORCE_COLOR ,
deliberately: an exported
.B NO_COLOR
is a statement about this machine, where a
.B FORCE_COLOR
inherited from a parent process is not.
.TP
.B FORCE_COLOR
.BR 0 " and " false
mean never;
an empty value,
.BR true ", and " 1 " to " 3
mean always. Any other value decides nothing and the next rule in the order
above applies.
.TP
.B MDVIEW_HYPERLINKS
.RB ( auto ", " always " or " never )
Whether to emit OSC\-8 hyperlinks, which are an axis of their own: they are
never emitted when standard output is not a terminal, whatever this or any
colour setting says, and
.B NO_COLOR
does not affect them. An unrecognized or empty value is ignored with one line
on standard error, exactly as for
.BR MDVIEW_COLOR ,
and is never read at all under
.BR \-\-plain .
.IP
Resolution order, highest first:
.B \-\-plain
(absolute off);
the non\-TTY ceiling (never on, off a terminal, whatever else is set);
.BR \-\-hyperlinks ;
this variable;
the config file's
.B hyperlinks
key; and finally terminal detection \(em on a terminal,
.B auto
asks what it advertises.
.TP
.B MDVIEW_GLYPHS
.RB ( ascii " or " unicode )
Overrides glyph\-tier auto\-detection when neither
.B \-\-ascii
nor
.B \-\-unicode
is given on the command line. Command\-line flags still take precedence over
this variable.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Standard XDG base\-directory variable; see FILES above for how it locates the
config file.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Standard XDG base\-directory variable; see FILES above for how it locates the
ambiguous\-width cache.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B non\-zero
An error occurred: a missing or unreadable input file, an invalid argument,
.B \-\-check
finding an over\-width line, or (for
.BR highlight\-as )
an unrecognized target language or a config file that could not be safely
updated.
.SH SEE ALSO
Project documentation in the
.B crucible\-tui
source repository describes the rendering model (list wrapping, table
allocation, glyph tiers) and the corpus\-sweep developer tooling in more
detail than belongs in this page.
